          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="kyo-answer-grid">
        <div class="kyo-answer-block"><h3>Kyota vs. Osaki</h3><p>Osaki delivers more features per dollar and a significantly wider model range. Kyota delivers a clearer upgrade ladder, stronger calf emphasis, and a more focused premium identity. Choose Kyota if you value knowing exactly what each tier adds; choose Osaki if maximum feature breadth or assertive dual-engine intensity is the primary driver.</p></div>
        <div class="kyo-answer-block"><h3>Kyota vs. Infinity</h3><p>Infinity is known for their Syner-D tandem-roller stretch profiles and a very wide lineup. Kyota offers a more structured premium ladder with clearer model-to-model distinctions. Infinity suits buyers who want stretch dominance; Kyota suits buyers who value clarity of choice and strong calf-and-foot work across the range.</p></div>
        <div class="kyo-answer-block"><h3>Kyota vs. Ogawa</h3><p>Ogawa leans aesthetic-first with a furniture-forward design philosophy and refined premium identities. Kyota is more function-and-ladder focused. Buyers who want their massage chair to feel like premium furniture often prefer Ogawa; buyers who want a clearly structured decision path, strong lower-body work, and AI-guided personalization often prefer Kyota.</p></div>
        <div class="kyo-answer-block"><h3>Kyota vs. Fujiiryoki</h3><p>Fujiiryoki is Japanese-heritage craftsmanship with refined, precise massage feel. Kyota is a clearly structured premium ladder with stronger calf emphasis and AI-guided personalization at the M900 tier. Fujiiryoki suits buyers who want Japanese precision and refinement; Kyota suits buyers who want a clear upgrade decision and strong lower-body performance.</p></div>
        <div class="kyo-answer-block"><h3>Kyota vs. Cozzia</h3><p>Cozzia offers their VarioMotion technology and a five-tier ladder. Kyota offers a similar tier structure with stronger calf work, 4D technology from the M780 upward, and AI body scanning at the M900. Both are solid premium choices; the decision often comes down to massage feel preference and which features matter most at each price point.</p></div>
        <div class="kyo-answer-block"><h3>Kyota vs. Bodyfriend</h3><p>Bodyfriend is Korean luxury with a strong medical-grade and wellness positioning. Their flagship systems are among the most powerful in the market. Kyota is more accessible in its premium identity and easier to navigate as a multi-model brand. Bodyfriend suits buyers who want maximum therapeutic intensity; Kyota suits buyers who want a clear, practical premium progression.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  {%- endif -%}

